4. **Redaction before spill but after threshold check** — redaction runs on `raw`, spill file gets redacted body; the `bytes` instruction = utf8 of redacted body (recomputed in buildUserSpillReplacement — yes it recomputes `utf8.encode(body).length` on the redacted body). Consistent.

5. **Preview of the replacement**: `buildPreview(body, ...)` with the redacted body — head+tail excerpts. Bounded via `headChars`/`tailChars` config (defaults 200/200). OK.

6. **`resolveRunSpillsConfig` change**: previously `if (value == null) return section; if (value <= 0) return const SpillsConfig(enabled: false);`. Now:
```dart
final value = int.tryParse(env[spillThresholdEnvVar]?.trim() ?? '');
final userValue = int.tryParse(env[userSpillThresholdEnvVar]?.trim() ?? '');
if (value == null && userValue == null) return section;
if (value != null && value <= 0) return const SpillsConfig(enabled: false);
final base = section ?? const SpillsConfig();
return SpillsConfig(
 enabled: true,
 threshold: value ?? base.threshold,
 ...
 userThreshold: userValue ?? base.userThreshold,
 userFraction: base.userFraction,
 ...
);
``
Subtle behavior change: previously, if `FAH_SPILL_THRESHOLD` was unset, the section was returned AS-IS (including `enabled: false` if configured). Now still the case when both null. But new: if ONLY `FAH_USER_SPILL_THRESHOLD` is set (and the tool knob unset), the function now returns `SpillsConfig(enabled: true, threshold: base.threshold, ...)` — it FORCE-ENABLES the spills section (`enabled: true`) even if the config section had `enabled: false`! Wait: if section had `enabled: false` and user sets `FAH_USER_SPILL_THRESHOLD=512`, then `value == null`, `userValue != null` → not both null → `value <= 0` false (value is null) → base = section → returns `enabled: true`, userThreshold 512. So setting the USER env knob re-enables spilling that the config explicitly disabled. Is that a regression? Compare with the pre-existing semantics for the tool knob: if `FAH_SPILL_THRESHOLD` set positive → `enabled: true` (env knob force-enables). That was the existing behavior for the tool knob — env knob overrides config including the kill switch (that's the documented behavior: "a bench/unattended run sets to enable/tune the tool-output cap for ONE run without any config file"). So the user knob behaving the same way is consistent with the tool knob semantics. Hmm, but is it intended that FAH_USER_SPILL_THRESHOLD=0 disables the user path but NOT force-enable: `userValue == 0`, `value == null` → both not null... `value != null && value <= 0` → false. Then returns `enabled: true`, userThreshold: 0 → isUserActive false, isActive true. So `FAH_USER_SPILL_THRESHOLD=0` with no config FORCE-ENABLES the tool-result path (enabled: true, threshold 8192 default). Previously with no config and no env, `resolveRunSpillsConfig` returned null (no section) → `attachSpillHooks` inactive. Now setting `FAH_USER_SPILL_THRESHOLD=0` to disable user spills also ACTIVATES tool-result spilling (enabled: true, default threshold 8192). That's a subtle side effect: a user who sets the env knob to turn OFF use